---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title='"Walk me through what happens when belongs_to :user is declared."' description="Practice card. A deep-dive interview question. Drills the senior-level 'I know what the framework actually does' answer.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/practice" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Practice
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Interview · Card 6</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">"Walk me through what happens when <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-3xl">belongs_to</code> runs."</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">A deep-dive question. The interviewer wants to hear that you know the framework, not just that you use it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The question
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">"When I write <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to :user</code> in a model, what does Rails actually do at class-load time? Be specific."</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: '"It adds a method called <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">user</code> that loads the associated user. That\'s it."',
              explanation: 'Names one of about ten things <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> does. The interviewer specifically asked for depth; this answer reads as junior.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: '"It defines a family of instance methods on the model — the reader, the writer, build/create variants, reload, and dirty-tracking helpers — plus class-level metadata in a reflection that other Rails internals (eager loading, form builder, fixtures) read. It also installs a presence validation by default in Rails 5+, controlled by <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to_required_by_default</code>. The dispatching point is <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">ActiveRecord::Associations::ClassMethods#belongs_to</code>, which delegates to the builder class <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">Associations::Builder::BelongsTo</code>."',
              explanation: 'Names: the multiple methods (reader, writer, build/create, reload, dirty tracking), the class metadata (reflection), the validation, the global setting that controls it, and the actual builder class involved. This is what senior depth sounds like. You don\'t have to recite all eight method names; you have to convey that you know the shape.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: '"It generates SQL to join the user table whenever you access the association."',
              explanation: 'No. The association is lazy by default — accessing <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">post.user</code> fires a separate query for the user. SQL JOINs only happen with <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">eager_load</code> or <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">joins</code>.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: '"It modifies the schema to add a foreign-key column."',
              explanation: 'No. The column is added by the migration. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> at the model level never touches the schema.',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>Deep-dive interview questions test whether you\'ve read the framework, not just used it. Two patterns that score:</p><ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-4 space-y-1"><li><strong>Name the file or class.</strong> "It\'s in <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">activerecord/lib/active_record/associations.rb</code>." Even if you\'re not 100% sure of the path, naming a plausible-sounding one shows you\'ve been there.</li><li><strong>Enumerate the surface.</strong> "It generates these methods, plus these class-level effects, plus this validation, plus these callbacks if you pass these options." A short list is enough; the point is showing you know there\'s a list.</li></ul><p>Bonus: name something about how the implementation is structured. "It hands off to a builder class" or "the validation comes from a separate method on the builder." Tells the interviewer you can read framework code.</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/source-belongs-to"
          theoryLabel="Reading the Source · What belongs_to :user actually adds"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-interview-hotwire" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 5</a>
      <a href="/practice/interview" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Back to Interview Practice →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
